AliasNull base userspace - versioned foundation artifact.

This is the first real, versioned AliasNull base-userspace artifact (Part 27-R,
extended by Part 27-S2, Part 27-T1 and Part 27-T2). It is NOT a Linux
filesystem, a rootfs image, a shell, a package manager, or a set of system
tools. It is a minimal, honest, self-identifying foundation that AliasNull
installs into its private runtime directory and verifies before the runtime
reports ready.

The artifact contains TWO real, source-built arm64 executables:

  aliasnull_base_probe - the first genuine executable component of the AliasNull
    base userspace (Part 27-S2). A small Android AArch64 PIE built from this
    repository (app/src/main/cpp/aliasnull_base_probe.cpp) with the Android
    NDK. It has two deterministic modes, selected solely by whether it is
    launched under the controlled base-execution environment (Part 27-T1):

      DEFAULT: prints "AliasNull base userspace OK" and exits 0 (unchanged
        since Part 27-S2 - the base-executable diagnostic asserts this line).

      CONTROLLED ENVIRONMENT: when the app launches it with the one
        AliasNull-owned environment override (ALIASNULL_BASE_ENV=<fixed
        marker>) and the controlled working directory under the verified base
        userspace, it prints "AliasNull base environment OK", then the real
        working directory ("cwd=<canonical path>") and the override echoed
        back, then exits 0. The base-environment diagnostic asserts those
        exact values, proving the controlled cwd and env were really applied.

  aliasnull_digest - the first genuinely REUSABLE real component of the base
    userspace (Part 27-T2): a dependency-free, read-only SHA-256 file-digest
    tool. A small Android AArch64 PIE built from this repository
    (app/src/main/cpp/aliasnull_digest.cpp) with the Android NDK; its SHA-256
    comes from a FIPS 180-4 implementation authored in that source, not from any
    cryptographic library, and it links only Bionic libc. It never executes a
    command. Its controlled mode is selected by the one AliasNull-owned
    environment override ALIASNULL_DIGEST_ROOT=<verified installed base root>:
    the component hashes exactly the installed base files (VERSION, ARCH,
    DESCRIPTION, PROVENANCE.txt, LICENSE.txt, aliasnull_base_probe,
    aliasnull_digest - the same files, in the same order, as the base manifest)
    and prints one deterministic "sha256  name" line per file, then exits 0.
    The Base Digest diagnostic asserts that output exactly, proving a real
    userspace executable independently reproduces the bootstrap's manifest
    digests for the whole installed base. The digest tool itself is not a
    general coreutils package and is not a Shell command; the app only ever
    runs its controlled mode, never an arbitrary file set.

It is NOT a shell, NOT a general command runner, and NOT a Linux userspace:
AliasNull does not yet include Python, Git, a package manager, or arbitrary
command execution, and unknown Shell commands stay unknown. The executables
are exercised only through the controlled developer-diagnostic native runtime
test panel (Base Executable / Base Environment / Base Digest), never through
the Shell command surface.

Contents of this artifact:
  VERSION              - the base artifact version (single source of truth)
  ARCH                 - the target architecture this build is produced for
  DESCRIPTION          - this description
  PROVENANCE.txt       - provenance and scope of every file in the artifact
  LICENSE.txt          - licensing note for the artifact files
  aliasnull_base_probe - the bundled 64-bit AArch64 base-userspace executable
  aliasnull_digest     - the bundled 64-bit AArch64 file-digest component

Version:   see VERSION
Arch:      see ARCH
Produced:  from the AliasNull repository and shipped inside the signed APK. The
           executables are built from committed source by CI with the Android
           NDK (see PROVENANCE.txt); their expected SHA-256 digests are
           recorded in the base-userspace artifact manifest and are not
           silently hard-coded.
